all:**\n*   I have Two Pair: Kings and Tens\n*   The board shows $10, Q, A, 9, K$, very dangerous!\n*   If the opponent has Jack, then I lose the chip.\n*   Checking is best.\n*   The valid action 0 gives best result.\n\n**The Ultimate Call:** My gut tells me to check. The risk of running into a straight is just too high on this scary board. With such a small pot, there's no need to bloat it up, especially against an opponent who might be trapping me. Checking back gives me the showdown with a good hand and wins me the pot. That seems like the smartest move here.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: I possess a strong Two Pair (Kings and Tens) with an Ace kicker. However, the community cards (10, Q, A, 9, K) are very connected, with four cards contributing to a Broadway straight. Any player holding a Jack has a Straight (10, J, Q, K, A), which beats my hand. The opponent has checked on every street, keeping the pot small. Betting here is risky because better hands (specifically any Jack) will certainly call or raise, while worse hands (like a lower pair) will likely fold given the scary board texture.\n\nReason: The board texture is dangerous as it allows for a "one-card straight" (any Jack). Since the opponent has checked the River, checking back (Action 0) is the safest play to get to the showdown and attempt to win the current pot without risking additional chips against a potential straight.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
