y.\n\n**My Decision:**\n\nBased on the hand evaluation and the opponent's likely range, checking seems like the most sensible play. Checking back allows me to maintain pot control and realize equity. I can check again to see if I improve on the turn or river. I'm choosing to check.\n\n**Formatting:**\nEvaluation: Checking is the safest move.\nReason: To keep the pot small with a marginal hand.\nAnswer: 0\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: You hold a pair of 7s on a flop of Queen-9-3. This gives you a made hand, but it is currently an underpair to the top two cards on the board (Queen and 9). The board texture is very dry (rainbow, unconnected), meaning there are few draws available. The opponent has checked to you, indicating weakness or pot control. Your hand has some showdown value but is vulnerable to overcards and stronger pairs.\n\nReason: Betting here yields little value; worse hands (like high cards) might fold, but better hands (any Q or 9) will call or raise. Checking allows you to control the size of the pot, potentially see a free turn card to improve to a set, and keeps your opponent's range wide including weaker hands you currently beat.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
